<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Aetolian League</h1><p class="st-meta">370 BCE – 189 BCE (approx.) · capital Thermon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federal state of the Aetolians of west-central Greece that became one of the great powers of Hellenistic Greece and controlled the oracle of Delphi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> lived in mountain villages north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, scorned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> as primitive. By 367 BCE an Aetolian federation was acting as a state, with its religious centre at the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermon">Thermon</a>. Its prestige soared when it helped repel the Gallic invasion of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a> in 279 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league had a general assembly of all citizens meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermon">Thermon</a> and elsewhere, a large council and an annual general (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>) assisted by a committee, the apokletoi. It expanded by granting citizenship or alliance to distant cities, controlled the votes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a> and was notorious for licensed raiding and piracy. Leaders such as Dorimachus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scopas">Scopas</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against Philip V in 211 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> felt cheated after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Cynoscephalae</a> in 197 BCE and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Defeated at Thermopylae in 191 BCE, the league accepted a peace in 189 BCE that left it a subordinate ally of Rome.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aetolian-league.jpg" alt="Aetolian League" width="320" height="185"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etolia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>370 BCE – 189 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thermon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dorimachus, Scopas, Phaeneas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (North-West Greek)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aetolian Confederacy, Koinon of the Aetolians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-280&amp;focus=aetolian-league" aria-label="Aetolian League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5218 793 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aetolian League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5538 1049L5559 1052M5538 1049L5556 1051M5538 1049L5519 1030M5538 1049L5304 942M5538 1049L5559 1052"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5559 1052h0M5556 1051h0M5519 1030h0M5304 942h0M5559 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5538 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5538 1049h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-280&amp;focus=aetolian-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

